Exactly right — in a real frontend, the user won't be typing MongoDB ObjectIds into a URL bar. Here's how it actually works in practice:

How it works with a real frontend
List page: You call GET /app/questions (your existing getAllQuestion route), which returns all questions — each one already includes its _id.
Frontend renders a list, something like:
jsx
{questions.map(q => (
  <div key={q._id} onClick={() => navigate(`/question/${q._id}`)}>
    {q.question}
  </div>
))}
The _id is already sitting in the data you fetched — the frontend just uses it silently in the background. The user just sees the question text and clicks it; they never see or type the ObjectId themselves.

Clicking navigates to a detail page/route like /question/6a52b0a527cbdd3fd16ecbed, and your frontend then calls:
GET /app/questions/6a52b0a527cbdd3fd16ecbed
using the _id it already had from step 1.

So in Postman, when you're testing manually
You're just doing step 3 by hand — copying an _id from the list response and pasting it into the URL, because you don't have a frontend yet to do that click-and-navigate step for you. That's completely normal at this stage of backend development.

